title: 'ASSESSMENT: what''s missing for Warpline to be day-one agent-managed source control for a new project'
summary: 'Four-agent panel (cold-agent empirical dogfood + Arky lifecycle + Loid teaching-layer + Jinx abandonment-risks) on the founder''s real question: can I start a new project and tell the agent team to build it on Warpline like git? Verdict: the adjudication ENGINE (fork→propose→admit→KNOT→resolve) is real, works, and its refusals/knot-payloads are unusually legible — the dogfood ran the full loop incl. a real cross-agent KNOT and human resolve on a throwaway project. But ''build it like any other project'' is blocked by a CHEAP usability/onboarding layer (fixable in days) sitting on top of DEEPER structural gaps (branching, N≥3 concurrency, blind-class honesty — weeks) and one EXISTENTIAL point: solo serial work produces zero contention, so the adjudication never fires and Warpline is pure additive friction over a git it can''t yet replace. The value only appears under real concurrency.'
